    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a 12-week hypertrophy block for a client but they've completely stalled on their back squat. They've hit a plateau where they can't add any weight for three weeks straight. Can you explain the physiological reasoning behind using a deload week here? Also, how should I approach volume manipulation to help them break through this? Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A deload is beneficial because sustained high-volume training leads to cumulative neuromuscular fatigue and micro-trauma, which can inhibit further strength gains. By reducing intensity and volume during a deload, you allow the central nervous system (CNS) and connective tissues adequate time to recover. To manipulate volume, try reducing the total working sets or decreasing the training frequency for the squat for one or two weeks. Following this recovery, carefully reintroduce intensity while perhaps using a slightly lower total volume initially to ease progression.",
      "source": "target",
