    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I was thinking about the CNS side of things. When a client hits a plateau like this, it feels like there's this invisible wall they just can't push through, even when their form is perfect. Is there a specific way to tell if it's mostly metabolic fatigue versus actual neurological burnout? And does adjusting the tempo or the rest periods help with that \"stagnant\" feeling in their progress?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Distinguishing between metabolic and neural fatigue can be tricky, but if performance drops severely across multiple lifts and effort feels incredibly draining, CNS burnout is probable. Metabolic fatigue is often more pronounced during high-rep sets where localized muscle burn is significant. Adjusting tempo, such as employing controlled eccentric movements, can enhance time under tension, which stresses different fibers and can break stagnation. Similarly, manipulating rest periods—perhaps lengthening them for maximal strength sets—can allow for better ATP-PCr recovery, providing more neural capacity for heavy lifts.",
      "source": "target",
